  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next system to bring rain showers and isolated \nthunderstorms arrives Saturday night and Sunday.\n\nA plume of subtropical moisture over the central Rockies this \nmorning will crest a central Plains ridge of high pressure today and \ntonight, carried eastward within a persistent west-east flow over \nthe Great Lakes region and reach the Lower Great Lakes by Saturday \nnight. This plume of moisture will increase PWats over our region to \naround 1.5 inches, or greater than the 90th percentile for Buffalo \nsounding climatology.  \n\nLift ahead of a few shortwave troughs aloft combined with lower \nlevel isentropic lift will generate a period of rain showers \nSaturday night through Sunday. Activity will advance northeastward \nacross the CWA with showers possible across the North Country by \ndaybreak Sunday morning.   \n\nThe axis of deeper instability will remain south of a frontal \nboundary that will remain draped across the midwest, leaving the \nthunder threat low. However the deepening warm cloud depth to over \n12K feet on Sunday combined with the anomalously high moisture \ncontent of the airmass will lend to heavy rain as the primary \nhazard. \n\nThese rain showers will shift to the east Sunday afternoon and \nevening, though a moisture rich lower atmosphere will remain Sunday \nand into the evening hours with higher terrain low stratus and fog \npossible. \n\nA broad area of surface high pressure over the province of Ontario \nwill advect a cooler and drier airmass southward later Sunday night \nand into Monday, possibly bringing a brief period of fair weather \nbefore another impulse returns the chances for showers and possible \nthunderstorms for Tuesday and Wednesday.",
